{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1519 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3a640ec34b78f7e0364dc207c42e9f7497d47e2d2cb75dac57b6d769f855a630" :edb/db-before-t 1518 :edb/db-after-t 1519 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534831 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:21:04.434Z" 13194139534831 true] [17592186195144 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195146 13194139534831 true] [17592186195149 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195151 13194139534831 true] [17592186195154 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195158 13194139534831 true] [17592186195159 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195160 13194139534831 true] [17592186195164 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195166 13194139534831 true] [17592186195324 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195325 13194139534831 true] [17592186195330 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195334 13194139534831 true] [17592186195336 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195340 13194139534831 true] [17592186195441 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195444 13194139534831 true] [17592186195448 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195450 13194139534831 true] [17592186198079 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/89fa15ffb9a7f6b319cf3b9ff0de4ab821a15f632d7f93618fbe99b7979260f4.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation." 13194139534831 true] [17592186198079 1100 "Describe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/9ec63ab02935633fe11a069f72b4098b.png)" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198079 1145 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y$, we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx \\in \\{1,2,3\\}, \\quad y \\in \\{-3,-2,-1,0,1,2 \\}\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad -3 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z^2} \\:: \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1} \\leq x \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3} \\leq y \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2} \\Big\\}\n$$" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198079 1145 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx \\in \\{1,2,3\\}, \\quad y \\in \\{ -3,-2,-1,0,1,2 \\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad -3 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{ (x,y)  \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z^2}\n\\: : \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}  \n\\leq  x \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3} , \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}  \n\\leq  y \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2} \n\\Big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198080 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/45260087c5196cc239f21402d73169f60139bd7d2c10ee0aa1db4e1a2c56be4a.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." 13194139534831 true] [17592186198080 1100 "Describe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/7cac8ef29134e3864a40c2aac8a76339.png)" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198080 1145 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=3$.\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\gt 2x, \\qquad y < 3\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y < 3\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y= 3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} 3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb R^2} \\:: \\: 2x \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt} y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} 3 \\Big\\}\n$$" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198080 1145 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\gt 2x, \\qquad y < 3.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y < 3.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y= 3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb R^2 } \\: : \\:  2x \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt } y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3 \\Big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198081 1100 "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/e5f5f2f2e67ba0ad566b2759ca5536d248f671bf0f082d567a7f7d993772601e.png)" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198081 1100 "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/fecea6c55850b5b4d4d702f2d38d5315.png)" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198081 1145 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{25} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(10,0)$. Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 \\gt 400\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400\n$$\n\nsince the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq} 400\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\:: \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16}x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25}y^2 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq} 400 \\bigg\\}\n$$" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198081 1145 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(10,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\gt 400.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 }x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25 }y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400 \\bigg\\}.\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198091 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/787b62fbe367feae9e4c8ed10b983a256840e3a93727fea7c1ff67cacad0129d.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." 13194139534831 true] [17592186198091 1100 "Describe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/592f96a01b0588aaacaa43f58d33b785.png)" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198091 1145 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y$, we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\nFor those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\lt x+2\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z ^2} \\:: \\: y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}x+2 \\Big\\}\n$$" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198091 1145 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x+2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\lt x+2\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z ^2} \\: : \\: y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2 \\Big\\}\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198401 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312958 13194139534831 true] [17592186198401 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313085 13194139534831 true] [17592186198401 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313113 13194139534831 true] [17592186198401 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313126 13194139534831 true] [17592186198402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313170 13194139534831 true] [17592186198402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313184 13194139534831 true] [17592186198402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313196 13194139534831 true] [17592186198402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313210 13194139534831 true] [17592186198403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312990 13194139534831 true] [17592186198403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313009 13194139534831 true] [17592186198403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313028 13194139534831 true] [17592186198403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313047 13194139534831 true] [17592186198404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313066 13194139534831 true] [17592186198404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313078 13194139534831 true] [17592186198404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313139 13194139534831 true] [17592186198404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313151 13194139534831 true] [17592186198404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313163 13194139534831 true] [17592186198699 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534831 true] [17592186198699 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/d550ecec3b7103c9510b6e45e738ae1385e07fa63ff2e50c54da6c17606faaec.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}\\le x\\le {{field-3}},\\,{{field-4}}\\le y\\le {{field-5}}}\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198699 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/q-181129.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$R$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}}" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198699 1145 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx\\in {-3,-2,-1},\\,y\\in {-4,-3,-2,-1}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-4\\le y\\le -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-4\\le y\\le -1}.\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198720 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534831 true] [17592186198720 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/0f95f6b259237816a5a3da041452cfd9458f0852b1ae717a044738b6f310ab5d.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2x+3}\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198720 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/q-181678.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$R$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198720 1145 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=3$ and *below* the line $y=2x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>3,\\,y<2x+3.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n3<y<2x+3.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=3$ and $y=2x+3$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\le y\\le 2x+3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le y\\le 2x+3}.\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198732 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534831 true] [17592186198732 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/cce16725dddcfc83098ee2323576b8e01bbc3f2625d17cb7e6030826ad9fbcd8.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,x-1}\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186198732 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/q-181983.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$Z$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139534831 false] [17592186198732 1145 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny<x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y<x-1}\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [