n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$}$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186313078 1113 #uuid "776a3516-03ea-5a65-aa0b-94e7bab490df" 13194139534831 true] [17592186313078 1145 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(0,5).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+4(5)^{2}\\,>\\,64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 64}.\n$$" 13194139534831 true] [17592186313079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313080 13194139534831 true] [17592186313079 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534831 true] [17592186313079 1041 "field-1" 13194139534831 true] [17592186313079 1104 #edb/ref 17592186313080 13194139534831 true] [17592186313079 1110 #uuid "8b8601b2-1afc-537f-a796-ab7867755b52" 13194139534831 true] [17592186313080 1034 "16" 13194139534831 true] [17592186313080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534831 true] [17592186313080 1111 #uuid "71a7f34f-5b40-545a-bfe9-9b55fda6dd80" 13194139534831 true] [17592186313081 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313082 13194139534831 true]